    <!-- <p class="text-teal-400 text-sm font-bold tracking-widest mb-6 font-['Barlow_Condensed']">PIVOT</p> -->
    <h1 class="text-teal-400 text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold font-['Barlow_Condensed'] mb-10 leading-tight">
      From FOH to Full Stack: Why I Became a Software Engineer
    </h1>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 text-gray-300">
      <p>For twelve years, my job was making sure nothing went wrong in front of thousands of people, live, with no second take. As a Lead Audio Systems Engineer at Live Nation, AEG Presents, and now at the MIM, I ran FOH and monitor systems for touring acts; which meant that when a signal chain failed mid-show, there was no "let me fix that and redeploy." I fixed it in seconds, while the room was watching.</p>
      <p>I didn't set out to become a software engineer. But somewhere across a decade of live production, I noticed the parts of the job I loved most weren't really about audio at all: they were about systems. Tracing a problem back through a signal path to find the one bad connection. Understanding how a dozen moving pieces depended on each other, and what would break if one of them didn't hold. Staying calm and methodical when something failed in real time, because panic never fixed anything, process did.</p>
      <p>That's the same muscle software engineering asks for. So in 2026, I went all in. I enrolled in Fullstack Academy, and finished with a 4.0 GPA, building projects in JavaScript, React, Node.js, Express, and PostgreSQL.</p>
      <p>This site is the first big project I built with that new skill set, from the ground up. I intentionally used it to test my chops with systems I hadn't touched before: Svelte, TypeScript, and Tailwind were all new to me going in, none of it overlapping with what I'd used in the bootcamp. But that's always been true of my career, in audio or in code: it doesn't matter what console, what PA, or what venue I'm working in, my engineering knowledge is what gets me through it. Signal flow is signal flow; the specific gear changes, but the underlying process translates. The same is true moving between frameworks. What actually gets me through learning something new isn't already knowing the tool, it's keeping a level head, following the process, and not quitting when it breaks the first ten times.</p>
      <p>It's also, in a way, still about audio: the "Bands" section running through the venues and acts I mixed for, the gear I used to know inside and out. I wanted the portfolio itself to be proof of the pivot, not just a description of it.</p>
      <p>I'm not leaving my old career behind so much as bringing it with me. Live production taught me to build things that have to work under pressure, to debug fast, and to take ownership when something's on the line. That's exactly the engineer I'm trying to be now.</p>
    </div>
